  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13941630/assets/ec6deda5b2400258af6ad455cdfa0072deb754391feb4d916082568c248d95ee.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y=k{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{x}.$ What is the value of $k?$",
  "worked_solution": "To plot the graph of $y=k{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{x}$ we take the graph of $y={(\\frac{4}{5})}^{x}$ and stretch it vertically by a scale factor of  $k.$ Under the action of this transformation, the $y$-intercept is mapped from $(0,1)$ to $(0,k).$\n\nComparing the point $(0,7)$ on the graph to $(0,k),$ we see that we must have $k=7.$",
